# findings.env — what this sandbox MEASURED, bound to the subject it measured.
#
# SOURCED by tests/ (treat it as code, not data — AUDIT F3).
#
# ── WHY A RECORDED RESULT NEEDS AN IDENTITY ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This repo's bug class #1 is a record that outlives the thing it describes. A file saying
# "Calico excludes ^br-.*" is exactly that shape: it stays readable, and merely stops being
# true, the moment the cluster under it changes version. Nothing errors — the record is
# simply false, and it surfaces far downstream wearing someone else's clothes.
#
# So the findings are stamped with the Calico version, the microk8s version AND the
# autodetection method they were produced by, and `tests/test-selection-rules.sh` REFUSES
# to compare a live run against them across a mismatch, printing both values. Same
# discipline as metal-as-a-service's `capture-policy`, which stamps `# image-sha256:` into
# `pcrs.expected` so a policy captured from a different build is refused by name.
#
# A mismatch is NOT a failure. It means the sandbox is now measuring a different subject
# and these numbers no longer describe it — re-run, read the result, and update this file
# in the same commit that changes what produced it.

# ── the subject ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NCS_CALICO_VERSION=v3.29.3
NCS_MICROK8S_VERSION=v1.35.6
NCS_AUTODETECTION_METHOD=first-found
NCS_MEASURED_ON=2026-08-07

# ── the findings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# rule 1 — the `^br-.*` exclusion is REAL, and this is the only line here that required a
# control to establish. With both decoys present Calico took `mc-decoy`; deleting it made
# Calico fall back to the INCUMBENT (a lower index) rather than to `br-decoy`, which was
# still up, still addressed, and at a HIGHER index. Ordering cannot explain that, so the
# only remaining difference is the name.
NCS_RULE1_BR_EXCLUSION=confirmed

# rule 2 — an addressed interface becomes a first-found candidate and Calico MIGRATES to it
# on its own poll, with nothing restarted. This is plan F.6's mechanism, reproduced on
# purpose instead of observed once as an outage.
NCS_RULE2_ADDRESSED_BECOMES_CANDIDATE=confirmed

# the ordering G.3 retracted an explanation for. Recorded as an OBSERVATION, not a law:
# among non-excluded candidates the later/higher index won. Consistent with the host, where
# Calico sits on `lxdbr0` (index 47) over an addressed uplink (index 2) — but one guest and
# one host are two data points, not a rule.
NCS_ORDERING_OBSERVED=higher-index-wins

# HOW LONG THE MIGRATION TOOK — recorded as a RANGE, because a single number was wrong.
#
# The hand-run of 2026-08-07 saw the incumbent still bound at ~100 s and the move complete
# by ~3 min. The FIRST packaged reproduction, hours later on the same image and the same
# Calico, saw it in **15 s**. Same subject, an order of magnitude apart.
#
# That discrepancy is the useful part. A record saying "180" would have been a fact that
# stopped describing its subject on the very first re-run — this lab's own bug class,
# committed inside the file that exists to prevent it. The honest statement is that the
# convergence time is VARIABLE and unbounded from above by anything we have measured, so a
# test must WAIT GENEROUSLY and must never sample once and call a slow migration a failure.
#
# Three runs so far: ~180 s (hand), 15 s, 10 s. The floor moved again on the third — which
# is itself the argument for a range rather than a number.
NCS_MIGRATION_SECONDS_MIN=10
NCS_MIGRATION_SECONDS_MAX=200

# ── G.9, closed on the real artifact (2026-08-07) ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# Rules 1 and 2 above were measured with DUMMY interfaces. A dummy in a guest is not a tap
# on `br-mc0`, and treating them as the same subject is the substitution this repo keeps
# getting caught by — so the scenario plan G.9 deferred in 2026-08-02 ("re-running F.6 means
# an outage on a live cluster") was run here on the REAL `fabric.sh`: its own tap, on its own
# bridge, given an address on purpose.
#
# The tunnel moved onto it. F.6's outage, reproduced deliberately, on the actual subject.
NCS_G9_FABRIC_TAP_CAPTURES_TUNNEL=confirmed

# And a portability finding that only a second host could produce: `fabric.sh` has UNDECLARED
# dependencies. It died instantly inside the sandbox with "dnsmasq not installed", and also
# needs `nft`. Five days of exercising it never revealed that, because the one machine it had
# ever run on happened to have both. The sandbox spec now installs them.
NCS_FABRIC_UNDECLARED_DEPS="dnsmasq nft"
